{
  "dom_id": "step-q121205",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "In a survey conducted among $100$ people, it was found that $38%$ of them have been in Canada, $70%$ have been in France, and $11%$ have been in both countries. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a person is selected at random among the $100$ people, what is the probability that he has been in Canada but not in France? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Clearly, $38$ people have been in Canada, $70$ people have been in France, and $11$ people have been to both countries.\n\nLet us call $C$ the event \"the person has been in Canada\" and $F$ the event \"the person has been in France.\"\n\nNote that there are $11$ people who have been in both countries. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $C$ and $F$ and write the number $11$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of people who have been in Canada *only*  is\n\n$$\n38-11=27,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $27$ in the part of circle $C$ that does not overlap with circle $F.$\n\n- The number of people who have been in France *only*  is\n\n$$\n70-11=59,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $59$ in the part of circle $F$ that does not overlap with circle $C.$\n\nThe total number of people who have been in Canada or France is\n\n$$\n27+11+59=97,\n$$\n\nand there are $100$ people in total, so the number of remaining people is\n\n$$\n100-97=3.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $3$ people who have not been in Canada or France, we write the number $3$ outside of the circles $C$ and $F.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14260559/assets/df1f2ed984115e59a15acf9bbcf1819713835ca24291aff423754ec5f4d579ad.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to people that have been to Canada but not in France. The probability that a randomly selected person is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap {F}^{′})=\\frac{27}{100}=0.27.\n$$",
